    "case_summary": "A 64-year-old man presented with mental status changes, abdominal pain, diarrhea, and worsening shortness of breath. Medical history included dyslipidemia, diabetes mellitus, hypertension, peripheral artery disease, and coronary artery disease with previous percutaneous coronary intervention to the left anterior descending and right coronary artery.\n\nPatient was febrile (Tmax 39.3°C [102.8 °F]), hypoxic with SpO₂ of 88% on room air, blood pressure of 125/62 mm Hg, heart rate of 98 beats/min, and respiratory rate of 18 breaths/min.\n\n- Laboratory findings:    - Leukocyte count: 13.4 × 10⁹/L (normal: 4.1–11.1 × 10⁹/L)    - Sodium: 129 mmol/L (normal: 135–146 mmol/L)    - High-sensitivity troponin: 102 μg/L (normal: <78 μg/L)    - N-terminal pro-brain natriuretic peptide: 1098 ng/L (normal: 0–900 ng/L)    - Positive Legionella pneumophila serogroup 1 antigen in urine    - Negative SARS-CoV-2 and influenza tests  - Imaging Studies:    - Chest radiograph: Right-sided lung infiltrates    - ECG findings (Figure 1): Sinus tachycardia with old incomplete right bundle branch block, new left posterior fascicular block, multifocal PVCs, atrial fibrillation with rapid ventricular response, fascicular ventricular tachycardia    - Echocardiography: LVEF 50%, no regional wall motion or valvular abnormalities    - Coronary angiography: Stable findings, no significant changes from previous    - Cardiac MRI findings (Figure 2):      - LVEF 46%      - Moderate subendocardial LGE in multiple segments      - Subepicardial LGE in apical inferior wall and papillary muscles    - 18F-FDG-PET/CT findings (Figure 3):      - LVEF 59%      - Perfusion defects in basal-mid-inferior/inferoseptal and mid-anteroseptal walls      - FDG uptake in posteromedial papillary muscle and anterolateral wall",
